0":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-65","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#why-byte-helpers-exist","content":"String-native formats do not pretend otherwise. Nanoid and CUID v2 have no canonical byte encoding, so they expose a smaller API."},"191":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-66","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#write-into-a-caller-owned-buffer","content":"Binary formats can also write directly into a buffer when an integration already owns the allocation:"},"192":{"id":"/docs/getting-started-67","page_id":"/docs/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/getting-started#write-into-a-caller-owned-buffer","content":"Public inputs are validated. Explicit random bytes and buffer offsets make tests and boundary integrations deterministic without changing the generator's default monotonic state."},"193":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","type":"page","content":"Choosing an ID","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Guides"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id"},"194":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-0","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","content":"Choose for the system boundary, not the trend."},"195":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-1","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id#a-good-default","content":"A good default"},"196":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-2","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id#storing-ids-in-a-database","content":"Storing IDs in a database"},"197":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-3","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id#skip-the-swap-flag-for-uuid-v7","content":"Skip the swap flag for UUID v7"},"198":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-4","page_id"