oosing-an-id","content":"MongoDB `_id` compatibility"},"219":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-25","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","content":"ObjectID"},"220":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-26","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","content":"Matches MongoDB's 12-byte ObjectID format."},"221":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-27","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","content":"Go rs/xid compatibility"},"222":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-28","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","content":"XID"},"223":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-29","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","content":"Matches rs/xid's compact 12-byte text and binary format."},"224":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-30","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","content":"Native `BIGINT` storage"},"225":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-31","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","content":"TSID"},"226":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-32","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","content":"A sortable 64-bit integer, not a UUID-shaped string."},"227":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-33","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","content":"Broad standards compatibility"},"228":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-34","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","content":"UUID v4"},"229":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-35","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","content":"The conventional random UUID format. Random values fragment primary key indexes."},"230":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-36","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","content":"An ID derived from something you already have"},"231":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-37","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","content":"UUID v5"},"232":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-38","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","content":"The same name and namespace always give the same UUID, with no state to keep."},"233":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-39","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id#a-good-default","content":"If you create records in more than one service or need an ID before inserting a row, start with UUID v7. It is a UUID-native value, sort-friendly, and uses a familiar database column type."},"234":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-40","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id#storing-ids-in-a-database","content":"A time-ordered ID keeps inserts at the end of an index only if the database compares the stored value in generation order. The stored width decides how large every index on the table gets."},"235":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-41","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id#storing-ids-in-a-database","content":"PostgreSQL has a native `uuid` type for UUID v7 and UUID v4 values. It stores the 128-bit value and compares it byte by byte, so UUID v7 rows sort by creation time. For ULID and other binary formats, the Drizzle example stores `toBytes()` output in a `bytea` column."},"236":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-42","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id#storing-ids-in-a-database","content":"MySQL has no UUID column type. Store UUID v7 and ULID values in a `BINARY(16)` column. Convert with `toBytes()` before you write and with `fromBytes()` after you read:"},"237":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-43","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id#storing-ids-in-a-database","content":"Avoid `CHAR(36)` primary keys in MySQL. InnoDB stores each table's rows in a clustered index ordered by the primary key, and every secondary index record repeats the primary key columns. A key of 36 bytes or more makes each of those indexes larger than a 16-byte key does."},"238":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-44","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id#skip-the-swap-flag-for-uuid-v7","content":"MySQL's `UUID_TO_BIN(id, 1)` swaps the first and third groups of hex digits before it stores the value. The swap exists for UUID v1, whose fastest-changing timestamp bits come first. UUID v7 already starts with its millisecond timestamp. The swap puts the third group, `7xxx`, in front. That group holds the version digit and 12 bits of a counter that uniku seeds with random bytes every millisecond, so the stored values stop following creation time."},"239":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-45","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id#skip-the-swap-flag-for-uuid-v7","content":"Use `UUID_TO_BIN(id)` without the flag, or bind `uuidv7.toBytes(id)` directly. Both store the same 16 bytes. A table that already uses the flag must also read with `BIN_TO_UUID(col, 1)`, because the flags have to match in both directions."},"240":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-46","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id#random-uuid-v4-primary-keys","content":"UUID v4 is a poor fit for an InnoDB primary key. Each insert lands at a random position in the clustered index, so InnoDB splits pages across the whole index instead of appending to the last one. The MySQL manual says pages filled in sequential order end up about 15/16 full, and pages filled in random order end up between 1/2 and 15/16 full. Use UUID v7 when the key must be a UUID. Use TSID when a `BIGINT` column works, since it takes 8 bytes."},"241":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-47","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id#an-id-derived-from-something-you-already-have","content":"Every other generator creates a new value on each call. Sometimes you need the opposite: an ID that is a pure function of an identifier you already hold, such as a session ID issued by a third-party tool. The same input must give the same output in another process, on another machine, or after a reinstall, with no lookup table to keep."},"242":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-48","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id#an-id-derived-from-something-you-already-have","content":"UUID v5 does this. It hashes a namespace and a name, so the function is the table:"},"243":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-49","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id#an-id-derived-from-something-you-already-have","content":"A UUID v5 has no timestamp and no useful order, and it is an identifier, not a secret: anyone who knows the name and namespace can compute it. See UUID v5 for namespaces, composing inputs, and string encoding."},"244":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-50","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id#time-ordering-is-local","content":"UUID v7, ULID, ObjectID, XID, and TSID carry time information. uniku keeps the state required for monotonic sequencing inside one JavaScript isolate; it does not coordinate clocks or sequences between processes or machines."},"245":{"id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id-51","page_id":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/choosing-an-id#time-order-leaks-creation-time","content":"UUID v7, ULID, KSUID, ObjectID, XID, and TSID embed a creation timestamp in the identifier itself. Anyone who can read the ID can decode when the record was created, not just its order relative to other records. That is a feature for index locality and a leak if the ID is public and the creation time is sensitive, for example a document, invoice, or account that should not reveal how old it is. If the identifier is exposed outside your system and creation time must stay private, use UUID v4, CUID v2, or Nanoid instead: none of them encode a timestamp."},"246":{"id":"/docs/guides/integrations","page_id":"/docs/guides/integrations","type":"page","content":"Integrations","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Guides"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/integrations"},"247":{"id":"/docs/guides/integrations-0","page_id":"/docs/guides/integrations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/integrations","content":"Runnable starting points for Hono, Drizzle ORM v1, and Effect v4."},"248":{"id":"/docs/guides/integrations-1","page_id":"/docs/guides/integrations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/integrations#hono-request-ids-with-nanoid","content":"Hono request IDs with Nanoid"},"249":{"id":"/docs/guides/integrations-2","page_id":"/docs/guides/integrations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/integrations#drizzle-orm-v1-with-binary-ulids","content":"Drizzle ORM v1 with binary ULIDs"},"250":{"id":"/docs/guides/integrations-3","page_id":"/docs/guides/integrations","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/integrations#effect-v4-services-with-typeid","content":"Effect v4 services with TypeID"},"251":{"id":"/docs/guides/integrations-4","page_id":"/docs/guides/integrations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/integrations","content":"The examples below add Nanoid request IDs to Hono, binary ULIDs to a Drizzle schema, and prefixed TypeIDs to an Effect service. Each is self-contained and runs with the command below it."},"252":{"id":"/docs/guides/integrations-5","page_id":"/docs/guides/integrations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/integrations#hono-request-ids-with-nanoid","content":"Hono's request ID middleware accepts a synchronous `generator`. When a request does not already carry a valid `X-Request-Id` header, this example generates a compact Nanoid, stores it in the request context, and returns it in the response header."},"253":{"id":"/docs/guides/integrations-6","page_id":"/docs/guides/integrations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/integrations#hono-request-ids-with-nanoid","content":"Run it with `pnpm --filter @uniku/examples example:hono`."},"254":{"id":"/docs/guides/integrations-7","page_id":"/docs/guides/integrations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/integrations#drizzle-orm-v1-with-binary-ulids","content":"The `binaryUlid` custom type exposes a string to application code while declaring a PostgreSQL `bytea` column. Its Drizzle codec converts ULID strings to `Uint8Array` parameters and selected bytes back to canonical ULID strings."},"255":{"id":"/docs/guides/integrations-8","page_id":"/docs/guides/integrations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/integrations#drizzle-orm-v1-with-binary-ulids","content":"The column's `$defaultFn` generates a ULID when an insert omits `id`. Both sides of the foreign key remain 16-byte values in PostgreSQL, so the join compares their binary representation while the returned `eventId` is already a string. The example uses an in-memory PGlite database, so it needs no external PostgreSQL server."},"256":{"id":"/docs/guides/integrations-9","page_id":"/docs/guides/integrations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/integrations#drizzle-orm-v1-with-binary-ulids","content":"Run it with `pnpm --filter @uniku/examples example:drizzle`."},"257":{"id":"/docs/guides/integrations-10","page_id":"/docs/guides/integrations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/integrations#drizzle-orm-v1-with-binary-ulids","content":"The example pins Drizzle ORM `1.0.0-rc.4`. Codecs are configured per PostgreSQL type, so this client treats every `bytea` column as a binary ULID. The v1 line is still a release candidate, so check its release notes before changing that version."},"258":{"id":"/docs/guides/integrations-11","page_id":"/docs/guides/integrations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/integrations#effect-v4-services-with-typeid","content":"An Effect service keeps ID generation injectable without leaking framework types into the generated value. This service exposes a traced `next()` effect and returns TypeID strings with a stable `user_` prefix."},"259":{"id":"/docs/guides/integrations-12","page_id":"/docs/guides/integrations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/integrations#effect-v4-services-with-typeid","content":"Run it with `pnpm --filter @uniku/examples example:effect`."},"260":{"id":"/docs/guides/integrations-13","page_id":"/docs/guides/integrations","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/integrations#effect-v4-services-with-typeid","content":"This example pins Effect `4.1.3`. Check the Effect release notes before changing that version."},"261":{"id":"/docs/guides/performance","page_id":"/docs/guides/performance","type":"page","content":"Performance and bundle size","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Guides"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/performance"},"262":{"id":"/docs/guides/performance-0","page_id":"/docs/guides/performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/performance","content":"Recorded generation benchmarks, measurement methodology, and the bundle cost of each import."},"263":{"id":"/docs/guides/performance-1","page_id":"/docs/guides/performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/performance#generation-speed","content":"Generation speed"},"264":{"id":"/docs/guides/performance-2","page_id":"/docs/guides/performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/performance#how-to-read-these-numbers","content":"How to read these numbers"},"265":{"id":"/docs/guides/performance-3","page_id":"/docs/guides/performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/performance#methodology","content":"Methodology"},"266":{"id":"/docs/guides/performance-4","page_id":"/docs/guides/performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/performance#bundle-size","content":"Bundle size"},"267":{"id":"/docs/guides/performance-5","page_id":"/docs/guides/performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/guides/performance#reproduce-these-numbers","content":"Reproduce these numbers"},"268":{"id":"/docs/guides/performance-6","page_id":"/docs/guides/performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/performance","content":"Generation speed is a design constraint in uniku, so the numbers are published\nrather than asserted. Results depend on the format: in this snapshot, Nanoid 6 and\nBSON ObjectID generate faster than uniku, while the other measured formats favor\nuniku. Application performance still depends on where and how IDs are created."},"269":{"id":"/docs/guides/performance-7","page_id":"/docs/guides/performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/performance","content":"Treat every ratio on this page as an order of magnitude, not a guarantee. Read\nhow to read these numbers before quoting one."},"270":{"id":"/docs/guides/performance-8","page_id":"/docs/guides/performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/performance#generation-speed","content":"The ranges below combine a four-repetition CI run\non November 15, 2026 (UTC) with repeat runs of the same command on different\nhardware. Node.js `25.9.0`, GitHub-hosted `ubuntu-latest` for the CI half. Each\nratio divides uniku's median throughput by the reference package's median\nthroughput; values above 1 favor uniku."},"271":{"id":"/docs/guides/performance-9","page_id":"/docs/guides/performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/performance#generation-speed","content":"Generator"},"272":{"id":"/docs/guides/performance-10","page_id":"/docs/guides/performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/performance#generation-speed","content":"Reference"},"273":{"id":"/docs/guides/performance-11","page_id":"/docs/guides/performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/performance#generation-speed","content":"uniku / reference"},"274":{"id":"/docs/guides/performance-12","page_id":"/docs/guides/performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/performance#generation-speed","content":"What the row actually compares"},"275":{"id":"/docs/guides/performance-13","page_id":"/docs/guides/performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/performance#generation-speed","content":"UUID v4"},"276":{"id":"/docs/guides/performance-14","page_id":"/docs/guides/performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/performance#generation-speed","content":"`uuid@14.0.3`"},"277":{"id":"/docs/guides/performance-15","page_id":"/docs/guides/performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/performance#generation-speed","content":"\\~1–1.6×"},"278":{"id":"/docs/guides/performance-16","page_id":"/docs/guides/performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/performance#generation-speed","content":"Same native primitive behind two wrappers — see below"},"279":{"id":"/docs/guides/performance-17","page_id":"/docs/guides/performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/performance#generation-speed","content":"UUID v5 (short name)"},"280":{"id":"/docs/guides/performance-18","page_id":"/docs/guides/performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/performance#generation-speed","content":"`uuid@14.0.3`"},"281":{"id":"/docs/guides/performance-19","page_id":"/docs/guides/performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/guides/pe