 upper bounds come from the linked CI run, which is published and\nauditable. The lower bounds and the repeat-run figures come from runs on a\nmaintainer machine (macOS arm64, Node.js `25.9.0`, same command, same commit) that\nare not published as artefacts. Re-running the commands below on your own hardware\nis the only way to get a number that applies to your hardware, and that is the\npoint — treat the table as a shape, not a scoreboard."],[653,"A parsed or supplied value exceeds the format's numeric range."],[337,"a regression must exceed `max(configured threshold, 2 × combined dispersion)`\nbefore it is reported, and reference-side rows never gate a change;"],[226,"A sortable 64-bit integer, not a UUID-shaped string."],[19,"A standalone binary (no Node.js required) is also available for macOS and Linux:"],[706,"A string name is encoded as UTF-8 and hashed as is. uniku does not apply Unicode normalization, which matches other implementations. `'é'` written as one code point (NFC) and as `e` plus a combining accent (NFD) gives two different IDs. If your inputs can arrive in either form, normalize them first:"],[678,"A string that is not a UUID throws a `ParseError` with code `INVALID_LENGTH`, `INVALID_FORMAT`, or `INVALID_CHAR`."],[707,"A string with a lone surrogate is not valid Unicode. UTF-8 encoding replaces each one with U+FFFD, so `'a\\uD800b'` and `'a\\uFFFDb'` give the same ID. The `uuid` package throws for these strings instead. If your names can contain arbitrary UTF-16, check them first with `String.prototype.isWellFormed()` where your runtime supports it."],[234,"A time-ordered ID keeps inserts at the end of an index only if the database compares the stored value in generation order. The stored width decides how large every index on the table gets."],[561,"A timestamp option is not an integer in the format's range, or a decoded timestamp overflows."],[625,"A timestamp was requested from a value that does not wrap a UUID v7: `uuidv7.timestamp()` was gi